{"version_id": 23, "article_id": 3, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Oil Companies Ask Supreme Court to Stop Climate Lawsuits", "title_zh": "石油公司请求最高法院叫停气候诉讼", "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/big-oil-asks-supreme-court-to-kill-climate-lawsuits-before-trial/", "image": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(42, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(80, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "96a25f2962ae"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 240, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "Oil companies went to the US Supreme Court on Monday. They want the court to stop more than two dozen climate lawsuits before they go to trial. The cases ask for billions of dollars to pay for damage from extreme weather.", "lead_zh": "周一，石油公司向美国最高法院提出请求，希望法院在开庭前叫停二十多起气候诉讼。这些案件要求石油公司赔偿数十亿美元，用于支付极端天气造成的损失。", "preview": "The Supreme Court heard arguments on Monday in a case brought by Boulder, Colorado, against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy. The city and count", "preview_zh": "周一，最高法院听取了科罗拉多州博尔德市起诉埃克森美孚和森科能源一案的辩论。该市和县于2018年起诉这些石油公司，称它们就气候变化欺骗公众。石油公司认为，联邦法律应阻止这些州和地方索赔。然而，博尔德表示，它只想要赔偿，而不是禁止石油公司的活动。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 13, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@5934804/content/audio/3/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 98.838, "size_bytes": 596736, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 274, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@5934804/content/data/versions/23.json", "paragraphs": ["The Supreme Court heard arguments on Monday in a case brought by Boulder, Colorado, against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy. The city and county sued the oil companies in 2018, saying they deceived the public about climate change. The oil companies argue that federal law should block these state and local claims. However, Boulder says it only wants money, not a ban on oil company activities.", "The oil companies' lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control global business. He warned that if Boulder wins, 90,000 towns could set energy policy. Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Many similar lawsuits have survived appeals, so oil companies are now asking the Supreme Court to stop them.", "The first question was whether the court should decide this case now. Some justices thought it might be too early. Other conservative justices seemed to agree that federal law, like the Clean Air Act, may block some claims. Justice Alito did not attend because he owns oil company stock. If the court ties, the case can move forward. A decision is expected by June.", "Supporters held rallies outside the court. Boulder's city manager said the city faces wildfires, floods, and extreme heat. She asked whether taxpayers must pay these costs alone. Many states and cities have paused their lawsuits until the Supreme Court decides. The ruling could end the cases or allow trials to begin."], "paragraphs_zh": ["周一，最高法院听取了科罗拉多州博尔德市起诉埃克森美孚和森科能源一案的辩论。该市和县于2018年起诉这些石油公司，称它们就气候变化欺骗公众。石油公司认为，联邦法律应阻止这些州和地方索赔。然而，博尔德表示，它只想要赔偿，而不是禁止石油公司的活动。", "石油公司的律师表示，博尔德试图利用州法律控制全球业务。他警告说，如果博尔德胜诉，9万个城镇都可以制定能源政策。博尔德的律师不同意。他说，此案与排放无关，而是关于欺骗。许多类似诉讼在多次上诉后仍存活，因此石油公司现在请求最高法院叫停它们。", "第一个问题是法院是否应该现在裁决此案。一些大法官认为可能为时过早。其他保守派大法官似乎同意，联邦法律（如《清洁空气法》）可能阻止某些索赔。阿利托大法官因持有石油公司股票而未出席。如果法院表决平局，案件可以继续推进。预计六月前作出裁决。", "支持者在法院外举行集会。博尔德市经理表示，该市每天都面临野火、洪水和极端高温。她质问纳税人是否必须独自承担这些成本。许多州和城市已暂停诉讼，等待最高法院裁决。该裁决可能终结这些案件，也可能让审判开始。"], "body": "The Supreme Court heard arguments on Monday in a case brought by Boulder, Colorado, against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy. The city and county sued the oil companies in 2018, saying they deceived the public about climate change. The oil companies argue that federal law should block these state and local claims. However, Boulder says it only wants money, not a ban on oil company activities.\n\nThe oil companies' lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control global business. He warned that if Boulder wins, 90,000 towns could set energy policy. Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Many similar lawsuits have survived appeals, so oil companies are now asking the Supreme Court to stop them.\n\nThe first question was whether the court should decide this case now. Some justices thought it might be too early. Other conservative justices seemed to agree that federal law, like the Clean Air Act, may block some claims. Justice Alito did not attend because he owns oil company stock. If the court ties, the case can move forward. A decision is expected by June.\n\nSupporters held rallies outside the court. Boulder's city manager said the city faces wildfires, floods, and extreme heat. She asked whether taxpayers must pay these costs alone. Many states and cities have paused their lawsuits until the Supreme Court decides. The ruling could end the cases or allow trials to begin.", "body_zh": "周一，最高法院听取了科罗拉多州博尔德市起诉埃克森美孚和森科能源一案的辩论。该市和县于2018年起诉这些石油公司，称它们就气候变化欺骗公众。石油公司认为，联邦法律应阻止这些州和地方索赔。然而，博尔德表示，它只想要赔偿，而不是禁止石油公司的活动。\n\n石油公司的律师表示，博尔德试图利用州法律控制全球业务。他警告说，如果博尔德胜诉，9万个城镇都可以制定能源政策。博尔德的律师不同意。他说，此案与排放无关，而是关于欺骗。许多类似诉讼在多次上诉后仍存活，因此石油公司现在请求最高法院叫停它们。\n\n第一个问题是法院是否应该现在裁决此案。一些大法官认为可能为时过早。其他保守派大法官似乎同意，联邦法律（如《清洁空气法》）可能阻止某些索赔。阿利托大法官因持有石油公司股票而未出席。如果法院表决平局，案件可以继续推进。预计六月前作出裁决。\n\n支持者在法院外举行集会。博尔德市经理表示，该市每天都面临野火、洪水和极端高温。她质问纳税人是否必须独自承担这些成本。许多州和城市已暂停诉讼，等待最高法院裁决。该裁决可能终结这些案件，也可能让审判开始。", "text": "The Supreme Court heard arguments on Monday in a case brought by Boulder, Colorado, against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy. The city and county sued the oil companies in 2018, saying they deceived the public about climate change. The oil companies argue that federal law should block these state and local claims. However, Boulder says it only wants money, not a ban on oil company activities. The oil companies' lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control global business. He warned that if Boulder wins, 90,000 towns could set energy policy. Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Many similar lawsuits have survived appeals, so oil companies are now asking the Supreme Court to stop them. The first question was whether the court should decide this case now. Some justices thought it might be too early. Other conservative justices seemed to agree that federal law, like the Clean Air Act, may block some claims. Justice Alito did not attend because he owns oil company stock. If the court ties, the case can move forward. A decision is expected by June. Supporters held rallies outside the court. Boulder's city manager said the city faces wildfires, floods, and extreme heat. She asked whether taxpayers must pay these costs alone. Many states and cities have paused their lawsuits until the Supreme Court decides. The ruling could end the cases or allow trials to begin.", "vocab": [{"word": "lawsuit", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诉讼", "note": "常用词，指法律案件"}, {"word": "deceive", "pos": "verb", "zh": "欺骗", "note": "指故意让人相信不真实的事"}, {"word": "federal law", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "联邦法律", "note": "指美国国家层面的法律"}, {"word": "preempt", "pos": "verb", "zh": "优先于；取代", "note": "法律用语，指联邦法优先于州法"}, {"word": "damages", "pos": "noun", "zh": "赔偿金", "note": "法律用语，指金钱赔偿"}, {"word": "emissions", "pos": "noun", "zh": "排放物", "note": "常指温室气体排放"}, {"word": "jurisdiction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "管辖权", "note": "法律用语，指法院审理案件的权力"}, {"word": "appeal", "pos": "noun", "zh": "上诉", "note": "指请求上级法院复审"}, {"word": "ruling", "pos": "noun", "zh": "裁决", "note": "指法官或法院的决定"}, {"word": "recuse", "pos": "verb", "zh": "回避", "note": "指法官因利益冲突退出案件"}], "title_original": "Big Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial", "summary_original": "Oil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.", "body_original": "Avoiding a reckoning\nBig Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial\nOil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.\nNicholas Kusnetz, Inside Climate News\n–\nOct 6, 2026 9:29 am\n|\n40\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nThe US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday that could help decide the fate of more than two dozen lawsuits accusing the industry of deceiving the public about climate change. The claims seek potentially billions of dollars in damages to help pay the costs imposed by climate-related extreme weather.\nYet the questions before the court did not focus on the merits of these claims but instead on technical legal matters over whether they should proceed at all.\nThe case being argued was brought in 2018 by the city and county of Boulder against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. While the case and dozens like it have survived multiple appeals in state and federal courts, the oil companies are asking the Supreme Court to declare that federal law preempts state and local governments from bringing such claims.\n“This case represents an unprecedented effort to use state law to regulate global conduct,” said Kannon Shanmugam, arguing for Exxon and Suncor. In effect, Shanmugam said, Boulder is attempting to use state law to regulate oil company activity around the world. “If Boulder’s claims are allowed to go forward, some 90,000 municipalities across the country would have the ability to make national and international energy policy by asking juries to impose catastrophic damages on selected fossil fuel producers.”\nBut Boulder and dozens of other plaintiffs have argued their claims do not attempt to impose any limits on oil companies at all—the Boulder case seeks damages, not an injunction on any activity.\n“This case is not about emissions,” said Kevin Russell, who argued the case for Boulder. “It’s about deception.”\nOil companies and conservative groups have been ramping up a campaign to block the lawsuits from proceeding as they inch closer to trial. Some states have enacted laws barring such lawsuits, and Republicans have introduced federal legislation that would shield oil companies from climate liability claims. The Trump administration has also intervened in several state cases, including Boulder’s, to try to block them from moving forward. Many scientists whose work is being cited in the cases\nhave been the subject\nof more than 100 public records requests from conservative groups seeking information about their work and funders, according to Politico.\nThe first question that arose at Monday’s hearing, posed by Justice Clarence Thomas, is whether the court has jurisdiction to hear the case at this stage because no ruling has been issued.\nThe oil companies contend that the proliferating lawsuits are already causing sufficient harm and that the court should decide now whether the cases should proceed. Several justices suggested they thought it might be too early to intervene.\n“I think Boulder’s best shot is on the jurisdictional question, whether this case is too early,” Michael Gerrard, faculty director at the Sabin Center for Climate Change Law at Columbia University, said in an interview.\nThe justices also probed the arguments of whether federal law preempts a state claim about climate change and emissions, either through the Clean Air Act or the Constitution.\nSeveral of the conservative justices seemed inclined to agree that federal law preempts at least some of Boulder’s claims, Gerrard said.\nThe oil companies’ lawyers have argued that the Clean Air Act preempts any claims against oil companies tied to climate pollution. They also argue that the Constitution itself gives the federal government exclusive control over interstate pollution.\nRussell argued that Boulder’s claim does not seek to limit pollution, and that there is nothing in the Clean Air Act or the Constitution that would bar states from taking actions that have a secondary effect of reducing pollution, such as imposing a gas tax or, in this case, seeking damages from oil companies.\nJustice Samuel Alito was not at the hearing after\nrecusing himself last week\n. While Alito gave no explanation for the decision, he had been under pressure to do so because he owns stock in oil companies that are party to other lawsuits that would be affected by the court’s decision.\nAlito’s absence leaves open the possibility of a tie vote, which would allow the case to move forward.\nSupporters of the case held rallies outside the court, calling on the justices to let it proceed.\n“Boulder, like other cities and counties across the country, is confronting realities associated with climate change every day, from wildfires and floods to drought, extreme heat, and growing pressures on our water and infrastructure,” said Boulder’s city manager, Nuria Rivera-Vandermyde, in a statement after the hearing. “The legal question before the court is whether taxpayers must bear these costs alone or must the defendants also pay their fair share for the harms they have caused.”\nMany conservative groups and politicians have filed briefs supporting the oil companies, and the Trump administration presented arguments to the justices on Monday.\nBoulder won supportive briefs from not only climate advocacy groups and Democrats but also many academics and former Environmental Protection Agency officials, including some appointed by Republican presidents.\nMany of the cases brought by states and local governments have been paused pending the outcome of the Supreme Court’s ruling in the Boulder case. The decision, expected by the end of the court’s term in June, could bring those claims to an abrupt halt or set the stage for trials to finally begin.\nNicholas Kusnetz is a reporter for Inside Climate News. Before joining ICN, he worked at the Center for Public Integrity and ProPublica. His work has won numerous awards and citations, including from the Society of Professional Journalists, the Society of Environmental Journalists, the Overseas Press Club, the Society of American Business Editors and Writers, and others. His articles have appeared in more than a dozen publications, including Wired, The Washington Post, Businessweek, The Nation, and The New York Times. Nicholas can be reached on Signal at nkusnetz.15.\nThis story originally appeared on\nInside Climate News\n.\nInside Climate News\nInside Climate News\n40 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-06T16:31:09.203604", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.282, "sm": 100, "em": 282, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Supreme", "s": 0.282, "e": 0.708, "sm": 282, "em": 708, "cs": 4, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "Court", "s": 0.708, "e": 1.012, "sm": 708, "em": 1012, "cs": 12, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "heard", "s": 1.025, "e": 1.275, "sm": 1025, "em": 1275, "cs": 18, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "arguments", "s": 1.288, "e": 1.788, "sm": 1287, "em": 1787, "cs": 24, "ce": 33, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "on", "s": 1.8, "e": 1.913, "sm": 1800, "em": 1912, "cs": 34, "ce": 36, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "Monday", "s": 1.925, "e": 2.4, "sm": 1925, "em": 2400, "cs": 37, "ce": 43, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "in", "s": 2.413, "e": 2.55, "sm": 2412, "em": 2550, "cs": 44, "ce": 46, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "a", "s": 2.562, "e": 2.625, "sm": 2562, "em": 2625, "cs": 47, "ce": 48, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "case", "s": 2.638, "e": 2.988, "sm": 2637, "em": 2987, "cs": 49, "ce": 53, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "brought", "s": 3.0, "e": 3.3, "sm": 3000, "em": 3300, "cs": 54, "ce": 61, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "by", "s": 3.312, "e": 3.438, "sm":